{% doc %}
  Authorization policy for the read-only MCP tools (pos-module-mcp spec 12.2).
  The engine has already proved the bearer token belongs to a real platformOS
  user; this is where the desk decides whether that user works here and whether
  their desk role may read tickets at all.

  modules/support/can/desk is called directly rather than through
  modules/user/helpers/can_do: can_do delegates to exactly this callback when
  one is given, and its own path first requires a roles array that an MCP
  identity does not carry.

  This decides who may call the tool. Which tickets they may then read is
  decided per call inside the handler, because that depends on the arguments.
  The engine also passes agent, arguments, tool and delegation_mode. This
  policy answers from the principal alone, so the app shim forwards only that:
  platformos-check reports a documented parameter a file never reads, and a
  policy that needs more should say so by taking it.

  Which tools an assistant may use is the engine's business, not this
  policy's: it narrows the served set per agent itself.
  @param {object} principal - The MCP principal, { id, user_id }
{% enddoc %}
{% liquid
  assign denied = { "allow": false, "reason": "not_a_team_member" }
  function actor = 'modules/support/mcp/helpers/actor', principal: principal
  if actor == blank
    return denied
  endif
  assign entity = { "member": actor.member }
  function allowed = 'modules/support/can/desk', requester: actor.profile, entity: entity, do: 'support.tickets.read'
  unless allowed
    assign denied['reason'] = 'desk_role_cannot_read'
    return denied
  endunless
  assign granted = { "allow": true, "reason": "desk_member" }
  return granted
%}
